PUBLIC RESPONSE: D

D is the only statement with demonstrable chronological and typological errors.

* Xiapei chronology/function. Xiapei/miaopei 霞帔 is a Zhou-Han derived ritual stole/sash, a rank insignia and ceremonial accessory of the fengguan-xiapei ensemble of Han-Wei-Tang-Song ritual/court dress. It is already in sharp decline after Tang and is essentially obsolete as everyday married-women’s wear by the Song-Yuan transition. Post-Song normative married women’s outer dress is the ruqun/aoqun complex → Ming beizi/shanshan/qixiong ruqun → Ming xiuhefu wedding set. Xiapei was not revived as, and never became, the standard attire for married women after the Song; the claim inverts its trajectory from active ritual insignia to obsolescence.

* Headdress taxonomy/anachronism. The elaborate metal-wire, kingfisher-feather, jewelled headdress with phoenix and bird motifs reserved for empresses/royal consorts/high nobility is the fengguan 凤冠, phoenix crown, a Ming-Qing codification. The Tang-Song first-ceremonial/folk head ornaments are the huaguan/hua guan/huagai floral/wreath crowns, a different class. D calls the Song-era floral crowns “referred to as the phoenix crown” and ties them to a post-Song xiapei standard, conflating huaguan with fengguan and projecting a Ming-Qing imperial crown back onto Song.

A, B, C and E remain defensible in broad received tradition:
A is the conventional Ming formalisation of the Han red wedding set fengguan-miaopei-hong sha with the dragon-phoenix couple shorthand;
B is the Zhou Li Six Rites veiling of the bride on departure from the natal home;
C is the well-attested Tang-Song fan-li face screening with a folding fan on leaving the natal home;
E is the Ming-inherited Han form persisting under the Qing: red silk inner ru under red embroidered xiuhe robe, wide red qun and red silk shoes with phoenix embroidery.

The period error on xiapei and the huaguan/fengguan conflation are unique to D.

